+# Layouts and views
+
+The arrays of a material can be stored component by component, or be
+owned by the calling code and described by views with arbitrary strides.
+This is described in a dedicated [page](views.html). In short:
+
+- `mgis::FieldView` describes the values of a group of variables at a set
+  of points, with a stride between two points and a stride between two
+  components. A null point stride describes uniform values;
+- the `layout` member of `MaterialDataManagerInitializer` selects the
+  layout of the arrays of a material data manager (`POINT_BY_POINT` by
+  default, or `COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT`). Everything given point by point to
+  the manager follows this layout. `save` and `restore` always use the
+  point by point layout;
+- `MaterialDataView` describes the data of a material stored in arrays of
+  the calling code. The functions of the material data managers
+  (integration, initialize functions, post-processings, end of time step,
+  extraction of internal state variables, conversions of the finite strain
+  stresses and tangent operators) also act on a material data view.
+  Without execution policy, they take a workspace
+  (`BehaviourIntegrationWorkSpace`), one per thread;
+- the rotations and the evaluation of the material properties also take
+  views;
+- `update`, `revert` and `updateValues` take operations copying and
+  filling views in the memory of the calling code (`FieldOperations`),
+  for instance on a device. Implementations are provided as headers for
+  `CUDA`, `HIP`, `SYCL`, `Kokkos` and `OpenMP` (`MGIS/FieldOperations/`);
+- `MaterialFunctionManager` takes an initializer, and gives strided
+  coalesced views of `mgis::function` for the managers stored component
+  by component;
+- the layout is exposed by the `C`, `Fortran` and `Python` bindings.
+
+The functions of the material data managers no longer allocate memory on
+the heap at each call in the usual cases, nor `evaluate`.
+
 # New features of the `MGIS/Behaviour` library
 
 ## Save/restore operations in `MaterialStateManager` and `MaterialDataManager`
