index 0000000..a181dd0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/web/views.md
@@ -0,0 +1,264 @@
+---
+title: Views on the data of a material
+date: 2026
+lang: en-EN
+numbersections: true
+documentclass: article
+from: markdown+tex_math_single_backslash
+geometry:
+  - margin=2cm
+papersize: a4
+link-citations: true
+colorlinks: true
+figPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+tabPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+secPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+eqnPrefixTemplate: "($$i$$)"
+---
+
+A material data manager owns the arrays of a material, stored point by
+point by default. A calling code may own its arrays: a finite element
+solver storing quadrature functions, or a code storing its arrays in the
+memory of a GPU, in a layout giving coalesced memory accesses. Those arrays
+can be described by views, with arbitrary strides, and given to the
+functions of `MGIS` without any copy.
+
+Views work with the libraries generated by the `generic` and the
+`generic-parallel` interfaces of `MFront` (see [this
+page](generic-parallel.html) for the latter).
+
+# Views on a field: `FieldView`
+
+`mgis::FieldView<ValueType>` (`MGIS/FieldView.hxx`) describes the values
+of a group of variables (or of a single variable) at a set of points. The
+value type is `real` or `const real`. The `j`-th component of the `i`-th
+point is stored at:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+v.data()[i * v.getPointStride() + j * v.getComponentStride()]
+~~~~
+
+This is the two-dimensional counterpart of `std::span`, with the meaning
+of a `std::mdspan` using the `layout_stride` mapping. A view does not own
+its values: copying a view copies a descriptor. A default constructed view
+is empty.
+
+Views are built by:
+
+| Function | Layout |
+|---|---|
+| `makeFieldView(values, layout, n, nc)` | dense, in the given `FieldLayout` |
+| `makePointByPointFieldView(values, n, nc, ps)` | components of a point contiguous, stride `ps` between two points |
+| `makeComponentByComponentFieldView(values, n, nc, ld)` | values of a component contiguous, stride `ld` between two components |
+| `makeUniformFieldView(values, n)` | the same values at each point (null point stride) |
+| `getComponents(view, offset, count)` | some components of a view, for instance a variable of a group |
+
+The `mgis::FieldLayout` enumeration has two values:
+
+- `POINT_BY_POINT`: the `j`-th component of the `i`-th point is stored at
+  `values[i * nc + j]`;
+- `COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT`: it is stored at `values[j * n + i]`. This
+  layout gives coalesced memory accesses on GPUs. It is the layout of the
+  "strided coalesced" views of `mgis::function`.
+
+The values must cover all the points and all the components. As for the
+views of `mgis::function`, a violation of this precondition is a contract
+violation (see `contract_violation_policy`): the program is aborted by
+default. The static function `FieldView::checkPreconditions` checks it
+beforehand.
+
+A uniform view (null point stride) can only be read. The values of an
+output must be distinct: `MGIS` checks it (`hasDistinctValues`).
+
+# Layout of a material data manager
+
+The arrays of a material data manager can be stored component by
+component:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+auto i = MaterialDataManagerInitializer{};
+i.layout = FieldLayout::COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT;  // POINT_BY_POINT by default
+auto m = MaterialDataManager(b, n, i);
+~~~~
+
+The layout is stored in the `layout` member of the material data manager
+and of its material state managers. Everything given point by point to the
+manager follows this layout:
+
+- the arrays of the states, the tangent operator blocks and the speed of
+  sound;
+- the external state variables with several components given by a
+  `std::span` (the material properties are scalars);
+- the inputs of the initialize functions and the outputs of the
+  post-processings given by a `std::span`;
+- the outputs of `extractInternalStateVariable` and of the conversions of
+  the finite strain stresses and tangent operators given by a `std::span`.
+
+The arrays are accessed through views in the layout of the manager:
+`getGradientsView(s)`, `getGradientView(s, name)`, and likewise for the
+thermodynamic forces and the internal state variables, and
+`getTangentOperatorBlocksView(m)`. `updateValues` refuses states of
+different layouts. `save` and `restore` always write and read the values
+point by point: a file does not depend on the layout.
+
+`MaterialFunctionManager` takes an initializer. With the
+`COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT` layout, its usual accessors refuse the variables
+with several components, and `getStridedCoalescedGradient<N>`,
+`getStridedCoalescedThermodynamicForce<N>` and
+`getStridedCoalescedInternalStateVariable<N>` return strided coalesced
+views of `mgis::function`.
+
+The layout is exposed by the `C`, `Fortran` and `Python` bindings. The
+`Python` bindings return `numpy` arrays with the strides of the layout.
+The two-dimensional accessors of the `Fortran` bindings refuse the
+`COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT` layout.
+
+# Material data views
+
+`MaterialDataView` (`MGIS/Behaviour/MaterialDataView.hxx`) is the
+counterpart of `MaterialDataManager` for arrays owned by the calling code.
+It holds no value:
+
+- `s0` and `s1` (`MaterialStateView`): views of the gradients, the
+  thermodynamic forces, the internal state variables and the energies,
+  and the views of the material properties, of the mass density and of the
+  external state variables;
+- `K`: tangent operator blocks, only used if a tangent or a prediction
+  operator is requested;
+- `speed_of_sound`: only used if the speed of sound is requested;
+- `batch_execution_policy`: policy used by the functions which do not take
+  one;
+- `memory_location`: location of all the arrays, checked against the
+  memory space of the behaviour.
+
+The material properties and the external state variables are given
+variable by variable, in the order of the behaviour, by
+`setMaterialProperty`, `setExternalStateVariable` and `setMassDensity`.
+Each one is uniform or given at each point. The vectors holding those
+views use a memory resource given to the constructor.
+
+`make_view(m)` returns a view on the arrays of a material data manager,
+with its layout, its execution policy and its memory location. The values
+held by the manager on the host are checked to be accessible by the
+computations of the behaviour.
+
+The functions check the consistency of a view at each call: behaviour and
+number of points of the states, number of views of the material
+properties and of the external state variables, extent of each view.
+
+# Functions on views
+
+| Function | Notes |
+|---|---|
+| `integrate`, `integrate_debug` | range of points `[b, e)` or all the points |
+| `executeInitializeFunction` | inputs given by a `FieldView` |
+| `executePostProcessing` | outputs given by a `FieldView` |
+| `rotateGradients`, `rotateThermodynamicForces`, `rotateTangentOperatorBlocks` | matrices given by a view or built from material axes, in place or not |
+| `evaluate` | values and inputs of a material property given by views |
+| `update`, `revert`, `updateValues` | with field operations (see below) |
+| `getGradientView`, `getThermodynamicForceView`, `getInternalStateVariableView`, `extractInternalStateVariable` | variables of a state view |
+| `convertFiniteStrainStress`, `convertFiniteStrainTangentOperator` | outputs given by a `FieldView`, on the host |
+
+The number of points is given by the views. The functions processing
+points have three forms:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+auto ws = BehaviourIntegrationWorkSpace(b);  // one per thread
+const auto r1 = integrate(ws, v, opts, dt);
+const auto r2 = integrate(policy, v, opts, dt);
+const auto pending = integrate(asynchronous_policy, v, opts, dt);
+~~~~
+
+- With a workspace, any library is accepted: a `generic-parallel` library
+  processes the batch with the policy of the view, a `generic` library is
+  integrated point by point on the host. If the components of a group of
+  variables are not contiguous, the values of a point are gathered in the
+  workspace, whose buffers are allocated once by its constructor.
+- With a synchronous or an asynchronous execution policy, the library must
+  be generated by the `generic-parallel` interface.
+- `integrate_debug` only takes a workspace.
+
+The rotations and the evaluations of material properties take no
+workspace: without policy, the `generic-parallel` libraries use a default
+policy, and the other ones compute point by point on the host. The
+overloads of the rotations taking arrays expect values stored point by
+point. They call the same functions on views.
+
+There is no form taking a `ThreadPool`: on the host, the parallelism is
+given by the `openmp` and `stlpar` backends, or by the calling code with
+one workspace per thread. The overloads taking an `IntegrationType` are
+only provided for the material data managers.
+
+The functions of the material data managers call the functions on views
+on a view of their arrays.
+
+# End of a time step: `FieldOperations`
+
+`update` and `revert` copy the arrays of a state in the arrays of the
+other state, and set the tangent operator blocks to zero. When the arrays
+are owned by the calling code, possibly in the memory of a device, the
+copies are delegated to an object implementing the `mgis::FieldOperations`
+interface (`MGIS/FieldOperations.hxx`), as the allocations are delegated
+to a `std::pmr::memory_resource`:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+auto ops = mgis::field_operations::CUDAFieldOperations{stream};
+update(v, ops);  // material data view
+update(m, ops);  // material data manager
+cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);
+~~~~
+
+- `HostFieldOperations` works in the memory of the host. It is used by the
+  overloads without operations.
+- Header-only implementations, compiled by the calling code with its own
+  GPU toolkit, are provided for `CUDA`, `HIP`, `SYCL`, `Kokkos` and
+  `OpenMP` (`MGIS/FieldOperations/`). With `CUDA`, `HIP` and `SYCL`, a
+  copy between two arrays with the same dense layout is performed by the
+  copy function of the runtime. The other copies, for instance a
+  transposition between the two layouts, use a kernel.
+- The operations may be asynchronous: the queue must be synchronized
+  before reading the values on the host.
+- `update` and `revert` of a material data view copy the gradients, the
+  thermodynamic forces, the internal state variables and the energies. The
+  material properties and the external state variables of a view belong to
+  the calling code.
+- For a material data manager, the values held by `MGIS` on the host
+  (uniform values, values stored with the `LOCAL_STORAGE` mode) are
+  updated on the host, the arrays of the calling code by the operations. A
+  copy between a value held on the host and an array of the calling code
+  is refused if the location of the arrays is not accessible by the host.
+
+# Example
+
+The arrays of a behaviour compiled for `CUDA` are stored component by
+component in the memory of the device, by the calling code. The end of the
+time step is performed on the device.
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+#include "MGIS/FieldView.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/FieldOperations/CUDAFieldOperations.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/Behaviour.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/MaterialDataView.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/Integrate.hxx"
+
+using namespace mgis::behaviour;
+const auto b =
+    load("libBehaviour-cuda.so", "Norton", Hypothesis::TRIDIMENSIONAL);
+const auto gs = getArraySize(b.gradients, b.hypothesis);
+auto v = MaterialDataView(b, n, mgis::MemoryLocation::DEVICE);
+// g0 and g1 point to arrays of gs * n values allocated by cudaMalloc
+v.s0.gradients = mgis::makeFieldView(
+    std::span(g0, gs * n), mgis::FieldLayout::COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT, n, gs);
+v.s1.gradients = mgis::makeFieldView(
+    std::span(g1, gs * n), mgis::FieldLayout::COMPONENT_BY_COMPONENT, n, gs);
+// ... thermodynamic forces, internal state variables, tangent operator
+// temperature: one value in the memory of the device, uniform
+const auto T = mgis::makeUniformFieldView(std::span<const mgis::real>(t, 1), n);
+setExternalStateVariable(v.s0, "Temperature", T);
+setExternalStateVariable(v.s1, "Temperature", T);
+auto policy = mgis::BatchExecutionPolicy{};
+policy.queue = stream;
+const auto r = integrate(policy, v, BehaviourIntegrationOptions{}, dt);
+auto ops = mgis::field_operations::CUDAFieldOperations{stream};
+update(v, ops);
+~~~~
